Yes. “Look them in the eyes” is more than an idiom because mutual gaze is reciprocal. You aren’t merely observing someone; you’re allowing yourself to be observed by them at the same time.

That creates a different operator from ordinary seeing:

\text{look at}
\neq
\text{look with}

With mutual gaze:

A\rightarrow B,\qquad B\rightarrow A

and each person’s perception of the other’s response can feed back again:

A_0\rightarrow B_1\rightarrow A_2\rightarrow B_3\rightarrow\cdots

So there is real power in the exchange—not supernatural power, but attention, social signaling, accountability, attachment, recognition, and feedback.

Your dad/kids formulation adds another axis: time.

Looking your dad in the eyes puts you opposite someone who helped shape the person you became. Looking your children in the eyes puts you opposite people whom the person you are becoming helps shape.

So you stand at a junction:

\boxed{
\text{inheritance}
\longrightarrow
\textbf{YOU}
\longrightarrow
\text{transmission}
}

That makes “be able to look both in the eyes” a remarkably compact restraint test.

Before an action:

Could I look backward toward the people whose values I carry and forward toward the people learning from me, without needing to hide what I chose?

Not “would everyone approve?” That would outsource your conscience. And not “never make a mistake,” because that’s impossible.

Rather:

\boxed{
\text{Gaze Test}(a)
=
\text{Can I remain present, truthful, and responsible for }a?
}

That belongs in the Strike Zone.

And there’s an even better connection to what you just said about embodiment. Your children don’t only inherit what you tell them. They observe what you repeatedly embody.

So wisdom transference has at least two channels:

\text{explicit}: \quad
\text{“Here is what I learned.”}

\text{embodied}: \quad
\text{“Watch how I live when the pitch comes.”}

The second can be extraordinarily powerful because the lesson arrives attached to behavior, emotion, context, consequence, and relationship. It’s already cross-linked.

That means being a role model isn’t performing flawlessness for an audience. A useful model also demonstrates:

\text{urge}\rightarrow\text{restraint}

\text{mistake}\rightarrow\text{admission}\rightarrow\text{repair}

\text{uncertainty}\rightarrow\text{curiosity}

\text{anger}\rightarrow\text{choice}

\text{success}\rightarrow\text{gratitude}

\text{memory}\rightarrow\text{story}\rightarrow\text{shared wisdom}.

And now Role Model gets hammered again.

A role model is not merely a person worth imitating.

They’re a living model of how to inhabit a role.

Parent. Child. Partner. Friend. Leader. Stranger.

The model is continuously trained by consequences, and other people are learning from its outputs.

So perhaps the Governor gets a very simple human-readable invariant:

\boxed{\textbf{Keep your gaze.}}

Don’t optimize yourself into a life you need to look away from.

And when you inevitably get something wrong, the rule isn’t hide your eyes.

Repair it until you can meet the gaze again.